    {
      "type": "heading",
      "snippet": "Factors to Consider"
    },
    {
      "type": "list",
      "list": [
        {
          "snippet": "Roast Level: Light roasts tend to have higher acidity and highlight the bean's origin flavors (fruit, floral). Dark roasts are bolder, smokier, with lower acidity and notes of dark chocolate and molasses. Medium roasts offer a balance of flavor, bitterness, and acidity."
        },
        {
          "snippet": "Bean Origin: Different regions offer distinct flavor profiles. For example, Ethiopian coffees often have fruity and floral notes, while Colombian beans tend to have a rich, chocolatey, and caramel character."
        },
        {
          "snippet": "Single-Origin vs. Blend: Single-origin coffees showcase specific regional characteristics, which can vary by harvest season. Blends are curated to provide a consistent, balanced flavor profile year-round."
        },
        {
          "snippet": "Whole Bean vs. Ground: Grinding beans immediately before brewing ensures maximum freshness and flavor."
        },
        {
          "snippet": "Brewing Method: Some coffees are better suited for specific methods; for instance, light roasts are often ideal for pour-over, while medium-dark roasts are very forgiving and work well for espresso."
        }
      ],
      "reference_indexes": [
